## Generic derivation of the pivot spine (not a generic unit certificate)

The *existence and location of the constant pivots* can be derived for an
arbitrary positive integer (t); this statement is stronger than merely
interpolating the three tables.  Put

\[
 q=2t+1,\qquad e=3t+1.
\]

Directly from

\[
 B=\pi(\pi-\gamma)+b_1\pi+b_2,\quad
 A=\pi B+b_3,\quad h=\pi A+b_4
\]

one obtains the exact identities

\[
 J(h,A)=\pi h-b_4\pi,
 \qquad J(h,B)=2h-b_3\pi-2b_4.
\]

In the banded Jacobian identity, the pair consisting of the leading term
(h^q) of (Q) and the term
(alpha_i h^{e-i}) of (P) contributes

\[
 qJ(h,\alpha_i)h^{e+q-i-1}.
\]

After monic (h)-division, its quotient contributes in band
(h^{e+q-i}): coefficient (q) on the (pi)-row for the (A)-coordinate
of (alpha_i), and coefficient (2q) on the constant row for its
(B)-coordinate.  No other term containing that same (alpha_i)-coordinate
can reach this band: pairing it with a nonleading (Q)-coefficient lowers the
band, while all other terms at this band use strictly smaller coefficient
indices.  Hence descending-band elimination gives constant pivots

- (q) on the (A)-coordinate for every (i=t+1,\ldots,e);
- (2q) on the (B)-coordinate for every (i=2t+1,\ldots,e).

The top coefficient (alpha_e), after its constant gauge, has coordinates
along (gamma,A,B,z), where (z=\pi-\gamma).  Its (A,B) coordinates are
included in the preceding list.  For the remaining two coordinates,

\[
\begin{aligned}
J(h,\gamma)&=-3b_1\pi^2-2b_2\pi-b_3
              +3\gamma\pi^2-4\pi^3,\\
J(h,z)&=3b_1\pi^2+2b_2\pi+b_3
              -3\gamma\pi^2+3\pi^3.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus, in band (h^{q-1}), the (pi^3)-row has coefficients
((-4q,3q)) on the ((\gamma,z))-coordinates.  Solving first for the
(gamma)-coordinate leaves coefficient (-3q/4) on the (z)-coordinate
in the (gamma\pi^2)-row.  These are field-constant pivots when
(operatorname{char} k\nmid6q).

There are therefore generically

\[
 (e-t)+(e-2t)+2=(2t+1)+(t+1)+2=3t+4
\]

constant pivots, in bands (4t+1,4t,\ldots,2t).  This proves a uniform
triangular *preprocessing recurrence*.  It does **not** show that the residual
ideal is the unit ideal uniformly in (t).

The three computed maps additionally give the same especially simple final
images.  If (u) is the (A)-coordinate of (eta_{t+1}) and (v) is the
(B)-coordinate of (eta_q), they are

\[
 \alpha_{e,\gamma}\longmapsto0,
 \qquad
 \alpha_{e,z}\longmapsto
 -\frac{t(t+1)e}{6q^3}u^3+\frac{te}{q^2}uv.
\]

The displayed coefficients equal
(inom{e/q}{3}) and (2\binom{e/q}{2}), as suggested by the formal
fractional-power recurrence, using (A^3=h^2z) and (AB=hz) after the lower
chart parameters are specialized to zero.  The full cancellation of all
other parameters has only been checked exactly at (t=2,3,4) in this lane;
accordingly this closed final-image formula is **MEASURED-PATTERN/CANDIDATE**,
not a generic theorem here.

